    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does squat strength improve vertical jump?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. Squat strength is one of the strongest predictors of vertical jump height. Research consistently shows that athletes who squat 1.5 to 2 times their bodyweight have significantly higher vertical jumps than untrained individuals. Increasing your squat max by 20 percent typically correlates with a 2 to 4 inch increase in vertical jump."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How much should I squat to jump higher?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "To meaningfully improve your vertical jump, aim to squat at least 1.5 times your bodyweight. Athletes who squat 2 times bodyweight or more tend to have elite vertical jumps. The relationship between squat strength and vertical jump is strongest when combined with plyometric training."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does squat strength affect sprint speed?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes, especially for acceleration over short distances like the 40-yard dash. Athletes with high relative squat strength apply more force into the ground during each stride. Research shows that athletes who squat 2 times bodyweight are typically 0.2 to 0.4 seconds faster in the 40-yard dash than athletes who squat 1.0 times bodyweight."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is a good squat for an athlete?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "For athletic performance, a squat of 1.5 times bodyweight is a solid foundation. Squatting 2.0 times bodyweight is considered strong for a male athlete and correlates with elite-level vertical jump and sprint performance. Female athletes at 1.5 times bodyweight are also considered very strong for their sport."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the average squat for a high school athlete?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The average high school male athlete squats approximately 1.0 to 1.3 times their bodyweight. Female high school athletes average around 0.8 to 1.1 times bodyweight. College-level athletes typically squat 1.4 to 1.8 times bodyweight, and elite athletes often exceed 2.0 times bodyweight."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Should I squat or do plyometrics to jump higher?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Both. Research shows the best vertical jump gains come from combining heavy squats with plyometric training. Squats build the force production capacity, while plyometrics train the rate of force development and stretch-shortening cycle. Either approach alone produces smaller gains than the two combined."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
